    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d384">

                <pb facs="0518" n="438" sameAs="frus:frus1901/pg438"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hay to Mr.
                            Thieriot.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1901-01-19"><hi rendition="#italic">January 19,
                        1901</hi>.</date></dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 32.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your No. 58, of the 24th ultimo, inclosing copy of a letter from the J. C.
                    Ayer Company, of Lowell, Mass., relative to the sale of their medicines in
                    Portugal.</p>
                <p>Referring to the Department’s No. 29, of the 20th ultimo, which crossed yours, I
                    have to say that you may use your good offices in the direction of one more
                    effort to obtain authorization for the sale in Portugal of certain of the
                    medicines of this company, although it would seem to the Department that the
                    efforts of this Government in its behalf had been well-nigh exhausted.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Hay.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
